{
  "passage": "The revolt of 1857 was the biggest challenge to British rule in India in the nineteenth century. It began as a rising of Indian soldiers, called sepoys, in the army of the East India Company. In 1857 the sepoys were given new cartridges that were said to be coated with the fat of cows and pigs. Both Hindu and Muslim soldiers were offended, because the cartridges had to be bitten open before use. On 29 March 1857, a young soldier named Mangal Pandey attacked his officers at Barrackpore and was later hanged. On 10 May, the sepoys at Meerut rebelled, freed their imprisoned comrades and marched to Delhi. There they proclaimed the old Mughal emperor, Bahadur Shah Zafar, as their leader. The revolt spread quickly to Kanpur, Lucknow, Jhansi and many other places. Nana Saheb led the rebels at Kanpur, Begum Hazrat Mahal at Lucknow, and Rani Lakshmibai fought bravely at Jhansi. Peasants, artisans and local rulers joined the soldiers. But the rebels lacked a single plan and modern weapons, and the British brought in more troops. Delhi was recaptured in September 1857, and Bahadur Shah Zafar was sent to Rangoon, where he died in 1862. In 1858 the British Parliament ended the rule of the East India Company and the British Crown took direct control of India.",
